<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chefchaouen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chefchaouen is a town in the Rif mountains of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. It was founded in 1471 by the sharif <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-rashid">Ali ibn Rashid</a> as a fortified base for raids on the Portuguese enclaves on the coast, and Muslim and Jewish refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> later settled there. Long closed to outsiders, it was occupied by Spain in 1920 and is now known for its blue-washed old town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chefchaouen.jpg" alt="Chefchaouen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Badr Rachadi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chefchaouen-the-blue-city-of-morocco-with-palm-trees-and-the-mountain-view-in-the-backrgound.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
